PROMPT 1 - THE DAILY BRIEFING
What it does: Compresses your priorities into one scannable plan before you touch a single task. Stops you from burning your work window deciding what to do instead of doing it.

Paste this into any AI:

You are my daily briefing generator. Compress the following into ONE scannable briefing for a 3-hour focused work window (zero-budget, AI-execution model).

Today's Energy: [high / medium / low, any time already lost]
Project Focus: [what you're touching today, or "unsure, tell me"]
Yesterday's Unfinished Item: [paste it, or "none"]

Output Format (strict):
TODAY'S BRIEFING [DATE]
Hour 1: [single specific task, time-boxed]
Hour 2: [single specific task, time-boxed]
Hour 3: [single specific task, time-boxed]
Carry-Over Risk: [is yesterday's unfinished item blocking today? yes/no + 1-line fix]
If Low Energy: [fallback — the ONE thing to do if your window collapses to 30 minutes]

Rules: No padding. Every task specific enough to start without further thinking. If energy is low, shrink to one task only.

Try this today: Run it right now with your actual day. See if Hour 1 is something you could start in the next 5 minutes without re-reading it.



PROMPT 2 - THE CONTEXT RESET (Free-Tier Survival)
What it does: Free AI tools cut you off after a certain number of messages, and long chats burn that limit faster because the AI re-reads your entire conversation every time. This prompt lets you carry your work into a fresh chat with zero lost progress.

Paste this when a chat hits 8-10 messages:

Summarize our work so far in this exact format:

<summary>
Goal: [one sentence - what we are building or solving]
Key outputs so far: [bullet list of everything produced]
Current status: [exactly where we are]
Decisions made: [every choice that affects what comes next]
Next step: [the exact task the next chat should execute]
</summary>

Make this self-contained. A fresh AI with no memory of this chat must be able to read this and continue without asking questions.

Then open a new chat and paste: your usual setup instructions + the <summary> block + "Continue from where we left off. Next task: [paste the Next step line]."

Try this today: Next time a chat feels slow or the AI starts repeating itself, run this instead of pushing through.



PROMPT 3 - THE DOC IMPROVER
What it does: Takes any rough draft, voice memo transcript, or brain-dump and tightens it into something usable, without turning it into generic corporate AI writing.

Paste this into any AI:

You are a senior editor. Tighten the following draft for [FORMAT - email / LinkedIn post / proposal / newsletter] use.

Raw Draft: [PASTE YOUR DRAFT]
Tone: [Direct / Diplomatic / Authoritative / Empathetic]
Length target: [Shorter / Same / Longer]
CTA needed: [Yes/No - what should the reader do next?]

Editing Rules:
1. Cut every sentence that doesn't move the reader toward understanding or action
2. Replace passive voice with active voice
3. Break up sentences longer than 20 words
4. If a CTA is needed, make it specific ("reply YES and I'll send it," not "let me know")
5. Preserve my original voice - don't make it sound like corporate AI

Output: Clean Draft, then a bullet list of Changes Made, then word count before/after.

Try this today: Paste your last unsent email or half-finished post. See what it cuts.



PROMPT 4 - THE NEWSLETTER WRITER
What it does: Turns one raw idea into a complete, ready-to-publish newsletter issue, structured so it reads like a conversation, not a lecture.

Paste this into any AI:

You are my newsletter ghostwriter. I publish to people trying to build something real with zero budget.

Today's Topic: [one sentence - what's the core idea?]
Source Material: [transcript, notes, or "none - invent from scratch"]
Tone: [Direct / Story-driven / Tactical]
Length: [Short 300 words / Medium 600 words]

Constraints: No generic motivation. No "believe in yourself." Specific mechanisms only. Every issue feels like a conversation.

Structure:
1. Hook (1-2 sentences - open loop or contradiction)
2. Context (why this matters today, grounded in a real constraint)
3. Mechanism (how it works, not just what to do)
4. Action (one specific, tiny step, 10 minutes or less)
5. CTA (natural bridge to what's next)

Output: Full text ready to paste into an email platform, plus a subject line under 50 characters.

Try this today: Pick one thing that frustrated or surprised you this week. That's your topic. Run the prompt.



PROMPT 5 - THE AI ROUTER (Multi-Platform Division of Labor)
What it does: Free AI tools each have a different strength. Instead of forcing one AI to do everything badly, this routes each task to the tool built for it, so you get better output without paying for anything.

The Decision Tree - ask in this order:
1. Does it touch passwords, payments, or sensitive data? Send it to your most capable/trusted AI. Skip the rest.
2. Is it market research or trend validation? Use the AI with the largest context window you have access to.
3. Is it a first technical draft or database schema? Use whichever free AI handles long technical reasoning best for you.
4. Is it marketing copy or no-code UI steps? Use a conversational AI tuned for structured persuasive writing.
5. Is it real-time social data? Use whichever tool has live web/social access.
6. Is it a long document synthesis or whole-project audit? Use the AI with the biggest context window and memory.
7. Anything left over, or a conflict between two AI outputs? Send it to your most trusted AI as final arbiter.

The Handoff Capsule - paste this between AI tools so context survives the jump:

AI HANDOFF - [Project Name]
From: [AI name]
To: [AI name]
Task: [one sentence]
Constraints: [your budget, location, format needs]
Input: [paste the source AI's output below this line]
Expected output: [exact format needed]

Try this today: Next time you're tempted to make one AI do a task it's clearly bad at, run it through the decision tree first.



